WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.947
The final coding exercise combines WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, and AVG.

00:00:04.947 --> 00:00:07.852
Use only Fall twenty twenty-four rows.

00:00:07.852 --> 00:00:10.717
That semester condition applies to source rows, so think about WHERE.

00:00:10.717 --> 00:00:14.342
Create one group per course.

00:00:14.342 --> 00:00:16.977
Calculate the average grade and enrollment.

00:00:16.977 --> 00:00:20.122
Then keep only courses with at least fifty students.

00:00:20.122 --> 00:00:24.817
Because enrollment is a grouped count, that threshold belongs after grouping.

00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:27.227
Sort with the highest average first.

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:29.777
Run and Check when your query is ready.

00:00:29.777 --> 00:00:35.062
If a course with fewer than fifty students still appears, inspect the HAVING condition.

00:00:35.062 --> 00:00:39.157
If Spring rows affect the average, inspect the WHERE condition.

00:00:39.157 --> 00:00:43.707
Use the meaning of each clause to debug the result.

00:00:43.707 --> 00:00:46.107
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:46.107 --> 00:00:51.142
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:51.142 --> 00:00:54.697
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:54.697 --> 00:00:58.837
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:58.837 --> 00:01:05.902
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:05.902 --> 00:01:10.072
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:10.072 --> 00:01:14.780
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
